---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const title = "Poola Investors - Crowd-sourcing is money plus action";
const description = "Poola's investor story: a unified crowd-sourcing platform for funds, skills, resources, volunteering, signatures, events and trusted campaign management.";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <section class="page-hero investor-hero">
    <div class="stripe-field stripe-field--small" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
    <div class="container page-hero__grid">
      <div data-reveal>
        <p class="signal"><span></span> Investor overview</p>
        <h1>A new interface for crowd-sourcing.</h1>
        <p class="hero__sub">Poola unifies fragmented crowd-funding categories into one platform where money, skills, time, resources, signatures, events and updates all compound into visible momentum.</p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="btn btn--primary" href="#funding" data-magnetic>What funding unlocks</a>
          <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="index.html#how-it-works">See the product</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="investor-snapshot" data-reveal="right" aria-label="Poola investor snapshot">
        <div><span>Initial wedge</span><strong>Social good</strong></div>
        <div><span>Expansion</span><strong>Creators + startups</strong></div>
        <div><span>Model</span><strong>2.9% + 6 SEK</strong></div>
        <div><span>Moat</span><strong>Funds + People Power</strong></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section investor-thesis">
    <div class="container narrative__grid">
      <div data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Market wedge</p>
        <h2>Fragmentation is the opening.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="copy-stack" data-reveal>
        <p>Today, supporters and starters are split across single-purpose platforms: donations, creator memberships, petitions, startup campaigns, events, volunteering and resources all live in separate places.</p>
        <p>Poola’s wedge is to start where emotional urgency and local networks are strongest, then expand into creators and startups with the same underlying crowd-sourcing engine.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="container market-grid" data-reveal-group>
      <article data-reveal><span>Social good</span><h3>Causes need trust and action.</h3><p>Donation campaigns often need volunteers, resources, signatures, updates and local awareness alongside funds.</p></article>
      <article data-reveal><span>Creators</span><h3>Creators need owned communities.</h3><p>Poola gives creators a way to activate fans beyond subscriptions, memberships or one-off project pages.</p></article>
      <article data-reveal><span>Startups</span><h3>Early teams need believers.</h3><p>Startup Pools can combine validation, funding, feedback, skills and audience-building in one place.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--color">
    <div class="container investor-model-grid">
      <div data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Business model</p>
        <h2>Simple fees, broader participation.</h2>
        <p class="lead">Poola earns on monetary contributions while leaving People Power free to grow the network. That keeps the experience fair for users and gives Poola more ways to retain them.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="investor-fee-card" data-reveal="right">
        <span>Unified fee</span>
        <strong>2.9% + 6 SEK</strong>
        <p>Per transaction. The same clear model across social good, creator and startup Pools.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container section-head center" data-reveal>
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--center">Product moat</p>
      <h2>The defensibility comes from repeated participation.</h2>
      <p class="lead">Features can be copied. A trusted network of Pool Starters, Poolers, updates, contributions and local action is much harder to replicate.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="container moat-grid" data-reveal-group>
      <article class="moat-card" data-reveal><i class="fa-solid fa-users-rays"></i><span>People Power</span><h3>More than money.</h3><p>Users can contribute skills, time, voice, resources and attendance when they cannot or should not only pay.</p></article>
      <article class="moat-card moat-card--dark" data-reveal><i class="fa-solid fa-layer-group"></i><span>OpenPools</span><h3>Category-level giving.</h3><p>OpenPools can reduce choice overload and give smaller campaigns a fairer chance to receive support.</p></article>
      <article class="moat-card" data-reveal><i class="fa-solid fa-shield-heart"></i><span>Trust system</span><h3>Safety as a feature.</h3><p>Verification, reporting, payments, updates and dashboards work together to make contribution feel safer.</p></article>
      <article class="moat-card moat-card--accent" data-reveal><i class="fa-solid fa-brain"></i><span>Pool Intelligence</span><h3>Guided campaigns.</h3><p>AI search, starter guidance, translation and fraud signals make the product more useful as activity grows.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section investor-competitive">
    <div class="container section-head center" data-reveal>
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--center">Competitive wedge</p>
      <h2>Poola can own the operational layer competitors leave open.</h2>
      <p class="lead">The product opportunity is not only collecting contributions. It is managing, proving and repeating them across money and People Power.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="container competitive-matrix" data-reveal>
      <div class="matrix-row matrix-row--head"><span>Capability</span><span>Typical crowdfunding</span><span>Poola direction</span></div>
      <div class="matrix-row"><span>Contribution model</span><span>Money-first pledge or donation</span><span>Funds plus skills, time, resources, attendance and voice</span></div>
      <div class="matrix-row"><span>After the pledge</span><span>Updates and comments</span><span>Commitment ledger, CRM, scheduling and next-action updates</span></div>
      <div class="matrix-row"><span>Distribution</span><span>Campaign link sharing</span><span>News widgets, OpenPools, partner matching and First Responder Pools</span></div>
      <div class="matrix-row"><span>Trust</span><span>Policy pages and reporting</span><span>Visible trust statuses, staged checks, reporting and proof updates</span></div>
      <div class="matrix-row"><span>Repeat use</span><span>New campaign, new trust cycle</span><span>Recurring support and a remembered Pooler relationship</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section launch-strategy">
    <div class="container roadmap-grid">
      <div data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Launch strategy</p>
        <h2>Start local, then compound.</h2>
        <p class="lead">Poola should avoid an empty global launch. The stronger path is to build dense local networks around social good, prove repeat behavior, then expand.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="roadmap" data-reveal-group>
        <article data-reveal><span>01</span><h3>Targeted networks</h3><p>Launch with curated local communities where social giving, volunteering and word-of-mouth already exist.</p></article>
        <article data-reveal><span>02</span><h3>Referral loops</h3><p>Use money-towards contributions, not cash-back discounts, so acquisition spend stays inside Poola.</p></article>
        <article data-reveal><span>03</span><h3>Contribution matching</h3><p>Let organisations use a slice of outreach budget to match donations and create visible momentum.</p></article>
        <article data-reveal><span>04</span><h3>Expansion sectors</h3><p>Scale the same platform mechanics into creator and startup Pools once the network has working proof.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--color moat-roadmap">
    <div class="container roadmap-grid">
      <div data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Feature moat roadmap</p>
        <h2>Turn features into network effects.</h2>
        <p class="lead">Each layer should make the next Pool easier to start, easier to trust and more likely to produce repeat Poolers.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="roadmap" data-reveal-group>
        <article data-reveal><span>Layer 1</span><h3>Starter operations</h3><p>Commitment management, CRM, action updates and volunteer scheduling make Pool Starters more successful.</p></article>
        <article data-reveal><span>Layer 2</span><h3>Trust visibility</h3><p>Transparent Pool states, fee clarity, reporting and proof updates reduce uncertainty before contribution.</p></article>
        <article data-reveal><span>Layer 3</span><h3>Distribution loops</h3><p>Widgets, matching, OpenPools and First Responder Pools bring high-intent contributors into the network.</p></article>
        <article data-reveal><span>Layer 4</span><h3>Repeat participation</h3><p>Late contributions, recurring support and Pool Intelligence turn one-off backing into a durable relationship.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section roadmap-section investor-roadmap">
    <div class="container roadmap-grid">
      <div data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Roadmap</p>
        <h2>Build the proof in stages.</h2>
        <p class="lead">Each stage should make the product more measurable, more trusted and easier for Pool Starters to explain.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="roadmap" data-reveal-group>
        <article data-reveal><span>Phase 1</span><h3>Trust-ready MVP</h3><p>Complete payments, reporting, profile trust signals and the core contribution flows.</p></article>
        <article data-reveal><span>Phase 2</span><h3>Curated launch Pools</h3><p>Bring the first social-good Pools online with strong stories, updates and local networks.</p></article>
        <article data-reveal><span>Phase 3</span><h3>Matching and widgets</h3><p>Test contribution matching, news widgets and referral loops that keep acquisition spend inside Poola.</p></article>
        <article data-reveal><span>Phase 4</span><h3>Expansion engine</h3><p>Extend the same platform mechanics into creator and startup Pools with Pool Intelligence layered on top.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section product-proof">
    <div class="container product-proof__grid">
      <div data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Proof direction</p>
        <h2>Make the product visible before the ask.</h2>
        <p class="lead">The strongest investor story is not another market-size chart. It is the product system that turns contributors into repeat participants.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="investor-proof-list" data-reveal-group>
        <article data-reveal><span>79 / 102</span><p>Archived MVP task board items marked done.</p></article>
        <article data-reveal><span>6</span><p>Contribution types: funds, skills, resources, volunteering, attendance and signatures.</p></article>
        <article data-reveal><span>3</span><p>Initial sectors: social good, creators and startups.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section funding-use" id="funding">
    <div class="container section-head center" data-reveal>
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--center">What funding unlocks</p>
      <h2>Turn the archive into launch evidence.</h2>
      <p class="lead">The next round should make Poola measurable: working payments, trusted onboarding, launch Pools, user feedback and a clearer path to repeat contribution.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="container funding-grid" data-reveal-group>
      <article data-reveal><i class="fa-solid fa-credit-card"></i><h3>Payments and payouts</h3><p>Complete the contribution flow, payout logic and finance controls needed for real Pools.</p></article>
      <article data-reveal><i class="fa-solid fa-mobile-screen"></i><h3>Store readiness</h3><p>Prepare iOS, Android and web paths so users can join from the devices they already use.</p></article>
      <article data-reveal><i class="fa-solid fa-shield"></i><h3>Trust and compliance</h3><p>Finish identity, reporting, safety and policy work before broad public launch.</p></article>
      <article data-reveal><i class="fa-solid fa-bullseye"></i><h3>Launch network</h3><p>Curate the first social-good Pools, local partners and contribution-matching experiments.</p></article>
      <article data-reveal><i class="fa-solid fa-chart-simple"></i><h3>Metrics and learning</h3><p>Track CAC, referrals, contribution frequency, repeat engagement and Pool success signals.</p></article>
      <article data-reveal><i class="fa-solid fa-wand-magic-sparkles"></i><h3>Pool Intelligence</h3><p>Prototype search, guidance, translation and safety features where they reduce real friction.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section final-cta">
    <div class="container final-cta__inner" data-reveal>
      <p class="signal"><span></span> Investor next step</p>
      <h2>See the product story, then the product.</h2>
      <p>Poola is being prepared as a trust-led crowd-sourcing platform for funds and People Power.</p>
      <div class="cta-actions">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="index.html#how-it-works" data-magnetic>See how it works</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost btn--on-dark" href="index.html#downloads">View downloads</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
